---
import { SectionHeading } from "@jj/ui";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

const repoTree = `jinawong-site/
├─ apps/site/                 Astro site (static output)
│  ├─ src/content/            case studies (Markdown + zod schema)
│  ├─ src/pages/              routes — /, /work, /about, /ui …
│  ├─ functions/api/          Cloudflare Pages Function (contact, off)
│  └─ wrangler.toml           Cloudflare Pages project config
├─ packages/ui/               React + TypeScript component library
│  ├─ src/tokens/             design tokens (CSS variables, light/dark)
│  ├─ src/components/         Button, Card, Tag, Timeline, …
│  └─ .storybook/             Storybook
├─ .claude/commands/          Claude Code slash commands
├─ .github/workflows/ci.yml   lint → typecheck → test → build → Lighthouse → preview
├─ CLAUDE.md                  conventions for humans and agents
└─ turbo.json                 task graph + caching`;

const pipeline = `pull request
   │
   ├─▶ lint         ESLint (TS, Astro, React hooks, jsx-a11y)
   ├─▶ typecheck    tsc + astro check  ◀── content schema runs here
   ├─▶ test         Vitest + Testing Library (packages/ui)
   ├─▶ build        astro build + storybook build
   ├─▶ lighthouse   perf ≥ 95 · a11y ≥ 95, or the PR fails
   └─▶ preview      Cloudflare Pages preview URL on the PR`;

const schema = `period: z.string().regex(/^(Mon )?YYYY( – (Mon YYYY|Present))?$/),
stack:   z.array(z.string()).min(2).max(10),
problem: z.string().min(40).max(500),
outcome: z.array(z.string()).min(1).max(5),
// z.strictObject: an unknown or misspelled key fails the build`;
---

<Base
  title="How this site is built"
  description="Monorepo, Astro islands, a tested component library, CI with Lighthouse budgets, Cloudflare Pages, and a Claude Code workflow for non-engineers."
>
  <article class="section container">
    <SectionHeading
      level={1}
      eyebrow="Colophon"
      title="How this site is built"
      description="Small site, team-sized setup. The point is the process: anyone on a team — engineer or not — can ship a change without breaking production."
    />

    <div class="prose body">
      <h2>One monorepo</h2>
      <p>
        pnpm workspaces and Turborepo, the same shape I use at work. The site and the component library live side by
        side; Turborepo runs lint, typecheck, test and build across both and caches what didn't change.
      </p>
      <pre aria-label="Repository structure"><code>{repoTree}</code></pre>

      <h2>Astro, with islands only where needed</h2>
      <p>
        Every page is pre-rendered to static HTML. React components from the library render on the server and ship{" "}
        <strong>zero JavaScript</strong> by default. Only the <a href="/ui/">component gallery</a> hydrates a React
        island, and only when it scrolls into view (<code>client:visible</code>). The theme toggle in the header is the
        library component's static HTML, wired by a few lines of framework-free script — no React on the page.
      </p>

      <h2>A component library with design tokens</h2>
      <p>
        <code>packages/ui</code> is a small React + TypeScript library: Button, Card, Tag, Timeline, MetricStat,
        SectionHeading and ThemeToggle. Styles are SCSS Modules that read only from design tokens — CSS variables for
        color, spacing and type, with light and dark themes. Each component has Storybook stories and Vitest + Testing
        Library tests that check behavior and accessibility (roles, names, <code>aria-pressed</code>), not snapshots.
      </p>

      <h2>Content with a schema</h2>
      <p>
        Case studies are Markdown files in an Astro Content Collection. A zod schema defines every field. A missing
        field, a wrong date format or a typo in a key fails <code>astro check</code> in CI, with a message that says
        which file and which field.
      </p>
      <pre aria-label="Excerpt of the content schema"><code>{schema}</code></pre>

      <h2>CI with budgets</h2>
      <p>
        GitHub Actions runs the whole pipeline on every pull request. Lighthouse CI enforces a performance and
        accessibility budget of 95 — a slow or inaccessible change fails like a failing test.
      </p>
      <pre aria-label="CI pipeline"><code>{pipeline}</code></pre>

      <h2>Cloudflare Pages</h2>
      <p>
        Static output deploys to Cloudflare Pages, with a preview URL for every pull request. A tiny Pages Function for
        a contact form is included behind Cloudflare Turnstile and stays switched off until it's configured.
      </p>

      <h2>Non-engineers ship safely, with Claude Code</h2>
      <p>
        The repo is set up so that someone who doesn't write code can still add a case study and get it live, with
        guardrails at every step:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          Open the repo in Claude Code and run <code>/new-case-study</code>. It asks a few plain-language questions and
          creates the file from a template.
        </li>
        <li>
          Claude follows <code>CLAUDE.md</code> — the house rules: where content goes, the schema, the tone, and what
          never to publish (private screenshots, internal code, secrets).
        </li>
        <li>
          It runs the same checks as CI locally (<code>pnpm check</code>) and fixes what fails before opening a pull
          request.
        </li>
        <li>CI repeats every check, and Cloudflare posts a preview link to review the real page.</li>
        <li>An engineer approves and merges. Production updates on merge.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        The written guide is <code>CONTRIBUTING-for-non-engineers.md</code> in the repo. Built with Claude Code,
        directed and reviewed by me — the same way I work day to day.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

<style lang="scss">
  .body {
    margin-top: var(--jj-space-6);
  }
</style>
